Use the existing window-handling methods already in `base_actions.py`/`page_.py` (`get_all_window_handles`, `switch_to_window`, `close_current_window`). Pattern is the same for both tab and window — Selenium treats both as "window handles."

```python
# Remember the original window
original_window = driver.current_window_handle

# ---------------- LINK 1 (opens new tab) ----------------
page.click()  # click link 1
all_windows = page.get_all_window_handles()
new_window = [w for w in all_windows if w != original_window][0]
page.switch_to_window(new_window)

page.wait_for_page_load()
assert "EXPECTED_TEXT" in page.get_text(), "Text mismatch in new tab"

page.close_current_window()
page.switch_to_window(original_window)

# ---------------- LINK 2 (opens new window) ----------------
page.click()  # click link 2 (make sure locator points to link 2 now)
all_windows = page.get_all_window_handles()
new_window = [w for w in all_windows if w != original_window][0]
page.switch_to_window(new_window)

page.wait_for_page_load()
assert "EXPECTED_TEXT" in page.get_text(), "Text mismatch in new window"

page.close_current_window()
page.switch_to_window(original_window)
```

Notes:
- `driver.current_window_handle` — you'll need `driver` directly (already available in your test file), since this isn't wrapped in `base_actions.py`
- Same logic works whether it's a tab or a separate OS window — Selenium doesn't distinguish
- Make sure your `page_.py`'s locator for "click" points to the correct link before each click (Link 1 vs Link 2), or use two separate locator slots if both links exist on the same page simultaneously
